#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Sign a CPAMC account in again without the usual fight.

Two things make CPAMC re-logins painful, and this tool removes both:
  - CPAMC's Codex sign-in link carries `prompt=login`, which makes OpenAI
    demand a full sign-in even when the browser is already signed in.
    The tool drops it.
  - One browser holds one OpenAI (or Claude) session at a time, so signing
    in as account B signs the browser out of account A. The tool opens every
    account in its own browser profile, which stays signed in to that account.
After the first sign-in in an account's profile, a re-login is one click on
"Continue".

Why accounts break: CPAMC's tokens belong to the browser sign-in they came
from (they carry its `session_id`). Signing out of that account in a browser,
or switching the browser to another account, revokes the session and OpenAI
then answers CPAMC with `token_revoked`. Keeping each account in its own
profile, and never signing out there, avoids that.

`--watch` (run by a systemd timer) catches the ones that break anyway: it takes
a revoked account out of CPAMC's rotation, so requests stop detouring through
it, and pops up a notification whose "Sign in" button runs this tool.

The sign-in itself is CPAMC's own (`cli-proxy-api -codex-login` and friends),
so CPAMC writes the fresh tokens. Afterwards the tool puts back the settings
CPAMC's file had (priority, prefix, ...), and moves aside an old copy if CPAMC
saved the new login under another name.

Usage:
  cpamc-login                     list accounts; ▲ marks the ones to sign in again
  cpamc-login broken              sign in every ▲ account, one after another
  cpamc-login 3                   account #3 (numbers from the list)
  cpamc-login alice               accounts whose email contains the text
  cpamc-login --add codex         add a new account (also: claude, antigravity)
  cpamc-login --no-open ...       print the sign-in link instead of opening a browser
  cpamc-login --json ...          one JSON result line (for the OmaAgents panel)
  cpamc-login --notify ...        desktop notifications (for clicks from the dashboard)
  cpamc-login --probe             check the tool can work, print a JSON status line
  cpamc-login --install-handler   let `cpamc-login://` links (the dashboard button) run this tool
  cpamc-login --watch             find revoked accounts, park them, offer a sign-in (systemd timer)
  cpamc-login --install-watch     install and start that timer (every 10 minutes)
"""

